  "query_preview": "(Today through Monday)...\nIssued at 259 AM EDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nStrong surface high pressure currently resides over southeastern \nCanada, providing brisk northeasterly flow to Indiana. Low stratus \nhas been gradually expanding beneath a strengthening inversion. To \nthe west, an upper-level low over Iowa is slowly moving \nsoutheastward.\n\nToday\n\nNortheasterly flow and overcast skies are expected to prevail today \nas the synoptic setup described above remains in place. Wind speeds \nmay increase a bit to 10-15kt this afternoon, but should generally \nremain around 10kt for the majority of the day into tonight. \nTemperatures will be muted by the persistent cloud cover, with highs \nin the upper 60s to near 70 anticipated.\n\nTonight into Wednesday\n\nThe upper-level low arrives late tonight which induces broad \ncyclonic curvature in the surface wind field. Combined with \nsteepening lapse rates aloft, this should generate broad lift. The \nadded lift will have two main effects. First, the stratus deck that \nis currently in place will thicken and lower, potentially to under \n1000ft agl. Second, patchy drizzle or light rain showers may develop \nafter 00z this evening, continuing into Wednesday. Best chance of \ndrizzle/rain showers is over the northeastern half of our CWA. \nBrief periods of reduced visibility are possible.\n\nSimilar to today, temperatures are expected to be reduced by the \ncloud cover. Another day with highs in the upper 60s to near 70 is \nlikely. Lows tonight and Wednesday night should be in the 50s to \nnear 60.\n\nThursday through Sunday\n\nThe aforementioned upper low will settle over the Mid Atlantic \nregion during the second half of the week. Cool E/NE flow and at \nleast some lingering cloud cover on the back side of the developing \ncutoff low will maintain below normal temperatures Thursday with \nhighs ranging from the upper 60s to the mid 70s from northeast to \nsouthwest. Ridging aloft will gradually shift into the Ohio Valley \nwith an axis of deepening subsidence advecting into the region from \nthe north. Under mainly clear skies...temperatures will recover back \nto late September normals with highs warming into the mid and upper \n70s by Sunday and perhaps into the lower 80s over parts of the \nforecast area by Monday. The coolest night since the Spring comes \nFriday night as lows in the mid and upper 40s will be common...with \ntemperatures recovering into the 50s for lows by the end of the \nweekend.",
